course: world
lesson: 178
title: The politician's either-or and the straw man
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Two tricks turn up in nearly every argument on TV, from every side: the either-or and the straw man.
explain: An either-or squeezes many choices down to two, and one of them is a disaster: "Either we build the new road, or our town will die." Ella's dad spots it: "There's a third option called 'fix the potholes'."
explain: A straw man is a scarecrow version of what your opponent said. She says "a bit less on the army", he answers "She wants no defence at all!" and knocks down the scarecrow. It works just the same the other way: "a bit more" becomes "He wants war!"
explain: Other tricks travel with them: attacking the person instead of the plan, changing the subject, "we've always done it this way", "everyone's talking about it", and the slippery slope "one small change and soon there'll be no rules at all".
explain: This lesson never says which side is right. It teaches you to hear the trick on every side, including the side you like.
fact: An either-or (grown-ups call it a false dilemma) offers only two choices when there are more; most problems have middle options.
fact: "If you're not with us, you're against children" is an either-or too: people can care about the same thing and disagree about how to help.
fact: A straw man changes what someone said into something more extreme, then attacks that instead of the real idea.
fact: Politicians on all sides use straw men; a fair debater first repeats the real idea, then says why they disagree.
fact: Many schools have middle rules about phones, such as phones switched off and kept in bags during lessons.
fact: Attacking the person (ad hominem) judges a plan by its maker's behaviour; behaviour can matter for trust, but it does not tell you whether a plan works.
fact: Answering a different question to dodge the real one is called a red herring; good interviewers ask the question again.
fact: "We've always done it this way" (an appeal to tradition) is not a reason on its own: old ways can be good or bad, so check whether they work.
fact: Being talked about does not make a plan good; people talk about bad ideas too.
fact: A slippery slope says one small step will lead to disaster without giving a reason for each step; many changes can be stopped or reversed.
fact: Words people use: "make it make sense" means "that doesn't add up"; "salty" means annoyed; "wicked" (New England) means very; "codswallop" (UK) means nonsense; "telly" (UK) means TV; "bare" (London) means lots of.
exercise: A politician says, "Either we build the new road, or our town will die." What is the trick? | Only two choices are given when there are more | The politician is attacking a person, not a plan | The politician is changing the subject to roads | None; there really are only two choices for the town
exercise: Ella's dad asks, "Is there nothing in between?" Which is a middle option? | Fixing the old road or adding a bus | Closing the whole town down at once | Building two new roads instead of one | Moving the whole town somewhere else
exercise: "If you're not with us on this, you're against children." Why is this unfair? | People can care about children and still disagree with the plan | Only people with children are allowed to vote | Everyone against the plan really is against children | No plan of any kind can help children
exercise: She says, "We should spend a bit less on the army." He replies, "She wants no defence at all!" What did he do? | Turned her idea into an extreme one and attacked that | Repeated exactly what she said, then disagreed politely | Agreed with her and moved on to the next topic | Asked her a fair question about her plan
exercise: A politician says, "We should spend a bit more on the army." His opponent says, "He wants war!" Which is true? | It's the same straw man trick, used from the other side | Only one side in politics ever uses straw men | Spending more on an army always means wanting a war soon | This one is fair, because it is about war
exercise: How does a fair debater answer the other side? | Repeat what they really said, then say why they disagree | Make the other idea sound as silly as possible, so they lose | Shout louder until the other side gives up | Talk about the other person's clothes instead
exercise: "Either we ban all phones in schools, or children will never learn anything again." What middle rule do many schools use? | Phones switched off and kept in bags during lessons | Phones out on every desk in every lesson | Phones handed in to the government at the start of each day | No middle rule exists in any school
exercise: "My opponent was rude to a waiter ten years ago, so his plan for schools is wrong." What is the trick? | Attacking the person instead of the plan | Giving only two choices | Changing the subject to football | Saying old ways are always best
exercise: Asked about hospitals, a politician talks about football for two minutes. What is this called? | A red herring: changing the subject | A straw man: twisting the question | A slippery slope: one step to disaster | A fair answer, because football is healthy
exercise: What do good interviewers do when a question is dodged? | Ask the question again | Change the subject too | Thank the politician warmly | Start talking about football
exercise: "We've always done it this way, so changing it would be crazy." What is the problem? | Being old isn't a reason by itself; check whether it works | Old ways are always wrong and must be dropped | New ways are always better than old ones | None; old ways must always be kept
exercise: "Everyone's talking about our new plan. That proves it's a great idea." Why doesn't it prove it? | People talk about bad ideas too | Plans nobody discusses are always the best | Talking about a plan makes it worse | Only quiet plans can ever work
exercise: "If we allow this small change, soon there'll be no rules at all!" What is this called? | A slippery slope | A false cause argument | A red herring trick | A tiny sample size
exercise: What should you ask when you hear a slippery slope? | What is the reason for each step? | How steep is the slope exactly, in degrees? | Who said it first on TV? | Which side is louder?
exercise: Which is true about these tricks in politics? | Every side uses them sometimes | Only the government ever uses them | Only the opposition ever uses them | They are banned in all TV debates
test: Teen: "So if I don't like your plan, I hate kids? I literally am a kid. Make it make sense." What has the teen spotted? | An either-or: agree with me or you're bad | A straw man about the army | A fair argument that he lost, so he's annoyed | A red herring about football
test: Gran: "A bit more money for the army means war now? Then if I buy a bigger umbrella, I want a flood." What is Gran laughing at? | The reasoning that twists "a bit more" into "war" | The politician's haircut and suit | Anyone who owns an umbrella | The idea of having an army at all
test: What would happen in a debate if both sides had to repeat the other's idea fairly before answering it? | They would argue about real ideas, not scarecrows | Nobody would be allowed to disagree about anything any more | The debate would have to stop at once | The same side would always win
test: Uncle from Boston: "That guy's wicked salty. He says she wants no army at all. She said a bit less!" Who is reasoning fairly? | The uncle; he repeats what she really said | The guy; "a bit less" really means none at all | Nobody; you can never know what she really said | The guy; he is angrier, so he must be right
test: Mum, at the telly: "That's lovely, love, but the question was about hospitals." What did the politician do? | Dodged the question by changing the subject | Answered the hospital question very well and fully | Made a slippery slope argument | Used a tiny sample of voters
test: In a cartoon a politician punches a scarecrow labelled "My opponent's plan" and the crowd cheers. What is the joke? | He is beating an idea nobody actually holds | Scarecrows are good at politics | The real opponent was a scarecrow all along | Punching scarecrows makes plans better
test: A kid says about "one small change and soon no rules at all": "Rules aren't Jenga." What does the kid mean? | One change doesn't topple everything unless each step has a reason | Rules are really made of wooden blocks | Changing any rule always ends in disaster | Jenga is the only game that has rules
test: Teen: "We always did it this way. We also always had no seatbelts, mate." What is the teen's point? | Old isn't automatically right; check whether it works | Seatbelts were a bad idea all along | Everything old should be thrown away | Politicians should all ride in cars
test: Grandad: "Everyone talked about my burnt Christmas turkey in 1998. Doesn't mean it was a great turkey." What does he show? | Being talked about doesn't make something good | Burnt turkey is the best kind of Christmas turkey | People only ever talk about things that are good | Christmas plans never work out, so stop making them
test: The politician you like best says, "Either my plan or total chaos!" What should you do? | Notice the either-or, even from your own side | Believe it, because it's your own side saying it | Check only the other side's tricks | Stop listening to politics for ever
test: Kid: "Rude to a waiter ten years ago, so his school plan's codswallop? Shouldn't we, like, read the plan?" What is the kid asking for? | Judging the plan by what is in it | Asking the waiter about schools | Voting for whoever is politest | Ignoring every plan about schools
test: Teen: "There's bare options between 'build the road' and 'the town dies', bruv." ("bruv" means mate.) What is she breaking? | An either-or | A straw man | A red herring | An appeal to tradition
exam: Identify the fallacy committed where a speaker asserts that only two courses of action exist, one of which is catastrophic, notwithstanding the existence of further alternatives. | A false dilemma | A straw man | A red herring | An appeal to tradition
exam: It is given that one politician proposed a modest reduction in military expenditure and that her opponent subsequently characterised the proposal as the abolition of all defence. Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the opponent's reply? | It attacks a distorted version of the proposal rather than the proposal itself | It restates the proposal accurately before disagreeing | It is an appeal to the popularity of the proposal | It is a legitimate inference from the proposal
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of such rhetorical devices in political debate. | They are employed by speakers on all sides | They are employed exclusively by governments | They are employed exclusively by oppositions | They are prohibited in all televised debates
exam: Ascertain the principal deficiency of the argument that a plan for schools is erroneous because its author was discourteous to a waiter a decade previously. | The author's conduct does not establish whether the plan would succeed | Discourtesy to waiters invariably indicates a defective plan | The plan was not concerned with schools | The incident occurred too recently to be relevant
